# aarch64 Limine boot-entry milestone wrapper.
#
# Two separate native-build conventions this file satisfies at once:
#
# 1. Boot-object autodiscovery keys off the ENTRY file's own directory: it
#    looks for a sibling `boot/` dir next to whatever .spl file is passed as
#    --entry (see link_objects_freestanding() in
#    src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/pipeline/native_project/linker.rs).
#    limine_boot_aarch64.spl's boot logic lives under src/os/kernel/boot/,
#    which is the wrong directory for that autodiscovery (its OWN sibling
#    `boot/` would resolve to src/os/kernel/boot/boot/, which doesn't
#    exist). This wrapper sits in
#    examples/09_embedded/simple_os/arch/aarch64/ so the sibling
#    examples/09_embedded/simple_os/arch/aarch64/boot/freestanding_runtime.c
#    gets discovered and linked in.
#
# 2. Entry-symbol aliasing: native-build compiles every top-level function
#    with its full module path mangled in (confirmed 2026-08-07: a function
#    named `kernel_main` here linked as
#    `examples__09_embedded__simple_os__arch__aarch64__limine_entry__kernel_main`,
#    NOT bare `kernel_main` — `ENTRY(kernel_main)` in linker_limine.ld
#    silently resolved to nothing, entry point 0x0, and --gc-sections then
#    discarded the entire reachable graph as unrooted, producing a "linked
#    successfully" 1 KB binary with no .text at all). The linker only
#    auto-generates a `--defsym=_start=<mangled sym>` / `--entry=_start`
#    alias for a top-level function literally named `_start` in the entry
#    file (see the `raw_start_candidate` derivation in linker.rs, matched
#    verbatim against this file's own mangled-path + `___start` pattern —
#    exactly mirroring examples/09_embedded/simple_os/arch/riscv64/entry.spl's
#    `fn _start():`). linker_limine.ld's ENTRY() must therefore say
#    `ENTRY(_start)`, not `ENTRY(kernel_main)`.

use os.kernel.boot.limine_boot_aarch64.{limine_aarch64_boot_main}

@noreturn
fn _start():
    """aarch64 Limine ELF entry point (see file header for why this must be
    named `_start`, not `kernel_main`). Limine hands off with EL1, MMU off,
    a valid stack, and no arguments — no assembly boot glue needed here,
    unlike riscv64/x86_64's `_start` (which set up their own stack/mode
    first)."""
    limine_aarch64_boot_main()
